2026-09-08|Total Commas Used from 1 to n

Question
- Given an integer n, return the total number of commas used when writing all integers from 1 to n in standard number formatting.
- A comma is inserted after every three digits from the right.

Approach
1. Numbers from 1 to 999 contain no commas, so they contribute 0.

2. Numbers from 1000 to 999999 contain exactly 1 comma.
   - If n is at least 1000, there are n - 999 such numbers.

3. Numbers from 1000000 to 999999999 contain exactly 2 commas.
   - There are n - 999999 of these numbers if n is at least 1000000.
   - Their contribution is 2 × count.

4. Numbers from 1000000000 to 999999999999 contain exactly 3 commas.

5. We can continue this pattern for every group of three additional digits.

6. For every threshold 10^(3k), all numbers from that threshold onward contain k commas.

7. Add the contribution of each range until the threshold becomes larger than n.

8. This approach takes only O(log n) time because we process digit groups rather than individual numbers.

Solution

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    long long countCommas(long long n) {
        long long ans = 0;
        long long start = 1000;
        long long commas = 1;

        while (start <= n) {
            long long end = start * 1000 - 1;

            if (end < start || end > n)
                end = n;

            ans += (end - start + 1) * commas;

            if (start > n / 1000)
                break;

            start *= 1000;
            commas++;
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
```

- Example: n = 1005
- Numbers 1 to 999 use 0 commas.
- Numbers 1000 to 1005 are 6 numbers.
- Each uses 1 comma.
- Total = 6.

- Time Complexity: O(log₁₀ n)
- Space Complexity: O(1)
